<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of the Geats</h1><p class="st-meta">500 CE – 1000 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The kingdom or kingdoms of the Geats in southern Sweden's Götaland, the people of Beowulf, later merged with the Svear to form Sweden.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Ptolemy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jordanes">Jordanes</a> mention Gautoi and Gautigoths in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>, and the Old English poem <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beowulf">Beowulf</a> makes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geats">Geats</a> and their king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hygelac">Hygelac</a> its heroes. Hygelac's raid on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frisia">Frisia</a>, recorded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-of-tours">Gregory of Tours</a> around 520, is one of the few datable events. Their heartland lay in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vastergotland">Västergötland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostergotland">Östergötland</a>, around lakes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-vanern">Vänern</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-vattern">Vättern</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Whether the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geats">Geats</a> formed one kingdom or several regional lordships is uncertain. Each land kept its own assembly and laws, later written down as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vastgotalagen">Västgötalagen</a>. Society was based on farming, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-technology">ironworking</a> and a warrior aristocracy, and the region kept close ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kattegat">Kattegat</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 1000 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geats">Geats</a> came under the kings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/svear">Svear</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olof-skotkonung">Olof Skötkonung</a>, traditionally baptised at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husaby">Husaby</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vastergotland">Västergötland</a>, ruled both peoples. Götaland remained a distinct part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-sweden">Kingdom of Sweden</a>, with its own lawmen, and its people sometimes elected rival kings into the 12th century.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-the-geats.jpg" alt="Kingdom of the Geats" width="320" height="729"><figcaption>Image: Lapplänning (highlighting by Lokal_Profil) · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sverigekarta-Landskap_V%C3%A4sterg%C3%B6tland.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>500 CE – 1000 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Norse paganism, later Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Norse</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/sweden">Sweden</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Götaland, Geatland, Gautland</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=750&amp;focus=kingdom-of-the-geats" aria-label="Kingdom of the Geats on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4952 225 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of the Geats lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5272 481L5381 440"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5381 440h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5272 481h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5272 481h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=750&amp;focus=kingdom-of-the-geats">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
